sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-30> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Computational irreducibility makes short theorems with only long proofs inevitable" ;
    dct:description "Because the proof processes of almost every area of mathematics are computationally irreducible, adding axioms cannot remove the problem: there will essentially always be theorems that are short to state but whose shortest proofs are very long." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “Implications for Mathematics and Its Foundations” (from p. 772), p. 779. Scope: axiom systems of mathematics, idealized as multiway systems" ;
    dct:type "mathematical" .
}

